'''Social information processing''' refers to how people notice, interpret and use information about other people and social situations. It also names distinct theories in developmental psychology and computer-mediated communication.

== Developmental social cognition ==
Crick and Dodge (1994) reformulated a model of social information processing involving encoding cues, interpreting them, clarifying goals, accessing or constructing responses, deciding among responses and enacting behavior. The model helps organize research on social adjustment and aggression, without implying a fixed conscious sequence in every encounter. Hostile-attribution measures require attention to context and developmental variation.

== Computer-mediated communication ==
Walther's 1992 social information processing theory addresses relational development under communication-channel constraints. Reduced nonverbal cues do not necessarily prevent interpersonal impressions; participants may adapt their communication over time. Time available, channel affordances, motivation and expectations matter. This is not the same model as Crick and Dodge's account of children's social adjustment.

== Experimental design and boundaries ==
Use standardized ambiguous scenarios and independently scored interpretations to examine cue attribution, then assess prospective behavior. For communication studies, vary modality and interaction duration while matching tasks and prior familiarity; measure relational judgments and observable coordination. Separate interpretation accuracy from self-reported confidence. Neither theory establishes that an AI reproducing text patterns experiences social cognition; engineered cue processing can be evaluated through task-specific predictions.

